=== layout: one-panel

# *Map Distance*

@body

- The map distance for tetrads is given by the formula\:
- with parentheses and below without\:

$$
\text{Map distance} = \frac{\frac{1}{2}(TT + 6 \cdot NPD)}{\text{total}}
$$

$$
\text{Map distance} = \frac{\frac{1}{2}TT + 3 \cdot NPD}{\text{total}}
$$

=== layout: section

# Tetrad Analysis with Three (3) Genes

=== layout: one-panel

# *Steps to Solve an Unordered Tetrad Problem*

@body

- _The yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae has unordered tetrads. A cross is made to study the linkage relationships among three genes._
- Step 1\: Find the Parental Ditypes (PD) for all three genes.
- Step 2\: Pick any two genes (for example, a,b)
- Step 3\: Determine if the two genes are linked.
  - PD &#8811; NPD &#8594; linked; PD &#8776; NPD &#8594; unlinked
- Step 4\: Determine the map distance between the two genes
  - D = &#189; (TT + 6 NPD)/total = (3 NPD + &#189;TT)/total x 100%
- Step 5\: Go to Step 2 and pick a new pair of genes until all pairs are complete.

